collab/exception-track/1794930000000-engineer-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:20:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yang.antlr.md | sed -n '3560,3770p;3838,3927p;3950,4078p;4105,4150p'\"","aggregated_output":"  3560\t```clojure\n  3561\t;; Illustrative contents of one task-owned Python function cell.\n  3562\t;; `entry` and `body-code` are live Yin closures, not literal AST maps.\n  3563\t{:yang.call/entry entry\n  3564\t :py/type :function\n  3565\t :spec parameter-spec\n  3566\t :defaults defaults\n  3567\t :code body-code}\n  3568\t```\n  3569\t\n  3570\tThe existing `:code` field is not the shared entry: its signature differs\n  3571\tbetween preludes. It may remain private. Cross-language callers must not\n  3572\tinspect it or extract it to bypass the entry.\n  3573\t\n  3574\tA cell-backed callable stores the entry in its cell contents. An\n  3575\timmutable callable descriptor, such as a bound method, may carry it\n  3576\tdirectly in its map. The common prelude projects the entry structurally:\n  3577\tread an existing sealed cell reference through `cell/get`, or inspect\n  3578\tthe immutable descriptor, then obtain `:yang.call/entry`. A\n  3579\tcell-ref-shaped map is dereferenced only when the heap authenticates it\n  3580\t(`cell/authentic?`, section 8.10.12); an unauthenticated shape projects\n  3581\tto itself and follows the ordinary descriptor rules, so data imitating\n  3582\tthe reference language stays data. The projection must not\n  3583\tdispatch on `:py/type`, `:js/type`, `:php/type`, language names, or a\n  3584\tregistry of languages.\n  3585\t\n  3586\tAn admitted callable is a runtime cell/descriptor constructed or exposed\n  3587\tby the callable constructors or import/export projections of a pinned,\n  3588\tconforming profile. The entry slot is a runtime descriptor field, not a\n  3589\tsource property that promotes arbitrary data to a function. A Python\n  3590\tdictionary, JavaScript object, or PHP array containing a source key\n  3591\tspelled `:yang.call/entry` remains ordinary source data unless an explicit\n  3592\tprofile-defined projection makes it a callable descriptor. Source\n  3593\tproperty access must not overwrite or masquerade as this runtime slot.\n  3594\tThis is an obligation of construction and source resolution, not a new\n  3595\tVM type, descriptor seal, or runtime provenance/language registry. Once\n  3596\tthe resolved descriptor is admitted, the shared projection is structural;\n  3597\tUAST deliberately constructing a conforming descriptor opts into the\n  3598\tconvention rather than changing a source language's data-map semantics.\n  3599\t\n  3600\tMissing entry means not callable. Constructors must install a genuine\n  3601\tunary Yin closure, and supported source mutations must preserve that\n  3602\tinvariant. Unary arity is a constructor/profile conformance obligation,\n  3603\tnot a signature introspection operation: Yin closures are opaque and the\n  3604\tVM's existing argument binding does not enforce this convention's arity.\n  3605\tA key in a map grants no VM privilege; application, ownership, and\n  3606\teffect checks still apply. A non-applicable entry remains an operational\n  3607\tVM application defect, not a source exception caught by the shared helper.\n  3608\t\n  3609\tFor callable kinds whose eligibility can change, the same cell or\n  3610\tdescriptor may also contain `:yang.call/eligible?`, a unary Yin predicate\n  3611\ton the original callable. Without it, an entry is eligible by default.\n  3612\tWith it, the common callable test uses its boolean result. The predicate\n  3613\treads explicit runtime metadata/heap only; it must not mutate state,\n  3614\tevaluate arguments, execute the target body, or invoke getters/user hooks.\n  3615\tIt has the same constructor/arity obligations as the entry. For example,\n  3616\tan instance can keep one dispatch entry while eligibility follows whether\n  3617\tits class currently supplies a supported call method; changing the class\n  3618\tdoes not require rewriting every instance.\n  3619\t\n  3620\tCall eligibility is tested at the callability-check point required by\n  3621\tthe caller's source, not universally before argument evaluation. The\n  3622\tentry resolves/rechecks a dynamic target at its source-required dispatch\n  3623\tpoint so argument side effects that change eligibility remain visible.\n  3624\tStatic callable kinds need no predicate.\n  3625\t\n  3626\tSource callable designators, such as PHP function-name strings and\n  3627\tcallable arrays where supported, are resolved under their source's rules\n  3628\tbefore common invocation. They do not gain entry slots merely by being\n  3629\tstrings or arrays. Exporting a designator as a first-class callable needs\n  3630\tan explicit frontend projection to an admitted descriptor, retaining any\n  3631\tsource-required late lookup and receiver semantics. Passing the raw\n  3632\tstring or array as data leaves it unchanged; a foreign caller must not\n  3633\tguess its language or search an ambient namespace to invoke it. This is\n  3634\tsource resolution/projection, not a language-pair wrapper around an\n  3635\talready admitted function.\n  3636\t\n  3637\tCross-language passage must not allocate a replacement function cell.\n  3638\tReturned functions retain their original identity, captures, defaults,\n  3639\tbound receiver, properties, and static state. A receiving frontend's\n  3640\tcallable test follows this common eligibility rule rather than rejecting\n  3641\tforeign native tags. Foreign attribute access and type reflection remain\n  3642\tprofile-owned; callability grants no general right to interpret another\n  3643\truntime's cell layout.\n  3644\t\n  3645\tCompiler-internal lambdas and host functions do not acquire this\n  3646\tconvention merely by being applicable in Yin. A source import exposing\n  3647\tone must emit an entry from its declared signature and effect profile.\n  3648\tIt must not guess an opaque closure's signature or bypass host-effect\n  3649\tand custody rules. Internal helper calls need not construct records.\n  3650\t\n  3651\t#### 8.10.3 Invocation record\n  3652\t\n  3653\tThe invocation is an ephemeral in-machine value. These keys are proposed\n  3654\tvocabulary, not existing repository APIs:\n  3655\t\n  3656\t```clojure\n  3657\t{:yang.call/callee     f\n  3658\t :yang.call/positional [v0 v1 ...]\n  3659\t :yang.call/named      [[\"name\" value] ...]\n  3660\t :yang.call/raise      raise-exit\n  3661\t ;; Optional: presence means a receiver was supplied, even when nil.\n  3662\t :yang.call/receiver   receiver\n  3663\t ;; Optional: pinned argument-binding extension, for example locations.\n  3664\t :yang.call/binding-profile \"<content-address>\"}\n  3665\t```\n  3666\t\n  3667\tThe four required fields mean:\n  3668\t\n  3669\t- `:yang.call/callee`: the original resolved callable, including its\n  3670\t  identity. The invocation helper supplies it; an extracted entry must\n  3671\t  not be applied to another object's substituted state.\n  3672\t- `:yang.call/positional`: a dense ordered vector of evaluated values.\n  3673\t  Its length records presence; no missing-argument sentinel is inserted.\n  3674\t- `:yang.call/named`: ordered name/value pairs, not a map. Names are\n  3675\t  plain text without caller-side case folding or normalization.\n  3676\t  Duplicate names remain visible until the callee's binder decides them.\n  3677\t- `:yang.call/raise`: this activation's caller-supplied, non-returning\n  3678\t  guest exit, accepting the condition in section 8.10.7. It closes over\n  3679\t  the caller's continuation and exception machinery, never a host\n  3680\t  callback or hidden global handler slot.\n  3681\t\n  3682\tAn absent receiver differs from one whose value is `nil`, `:py/None`,\n  3683\t`:php/null`, `:js/null`, or `:js/undefined`. Empty argument vectors are\n  3684\trequired rather than represented by absent keys. Presence must not be\n  3685\tinferred from truthiness.\n  3686\t\n  3687\tThe optional binding profile pins additional argument semantics. It\n  3688\tdoes not name the caller's language or select a language-pair bridge.\n  3689\tAbsent that extension, arguments are by-value runtime values as in\n  3690\tsection 8.10.6. The callee entry compares the supplied profile address\n  3691\twith its declared accepted/required binding profiles before acquiring\n  3692\treferences or entering the body. An unsupported required profile or a\n  3693\tmissing required extension fails there. The pinned profile defines the\n  3694\tshape and validation of any extra invocation fields it requires; values\n  3695\tin the base vectors alone do not establish a location protocol.\n  3696\t\n  3697\tThe PHP reference-argument profile is pinned as\n  3698\t`sha256:171656777d777865312132fe18cbc7a057a4721844a4dc450c0800381d2223eb`.\n  3699\tPHP call sites include it when their declared target has reference\n  3700\tparameters; corresponding positional values may then be PHP reference\n  3701\tmarkers for caller locations. The PHP entry rejects unknown profile\n  3702\taddresses before parameter binding, and only this profile authorizes its\n  3703\tbinder to attach a parameter to a caller marker. The markers alone carry\n  3704\tno binding authority.\n  3705\t\n  3706\tConvention revision is pinned by the existing lowering/runtime profiles.\n  3707\tIt is not a new canonical AST slot, a per-function language tag, or a\n  3708\trepeated version field in every invocation. Incompatible revisions need\n  3709\tnew profiles and explicit admission, never entry-shape guessing.\n  3710\t\n  3711\tThe shared helper validates the record's map shape, required presence,\n  3712\tpositional vector, and ordered pairs with text names. The callee entry\n  3713\tvalidates extension fields under its admitted binding profile; the shared\n  3714\thelper knows no extension-specific representation. A valid caller-owned\n  3715\traise exit is established before constructing the record, so structural\n  3716\tfailures can be transferred through it. If no valid raise exit exists,\n  3717\tthat is an operational wiring failure; do not invent a source exception\n  3718\tpath, default missing fields to `nil`, or broadly catch VM application\n  3719\terrors. Checking live entry/exit/predicate closure arity is not part of\n  3720\tthis validation; constructor fixtures and admission under pinned\n  3721\tprofiles establish those obligations.\n  3722\t\n  3723\t#### 8.10.4 Source semantics at caller and callee\n  3724\t\n  3725\tThe caller's lowering preserves its source rules for callable resolution,\n  3726\targument/receiver evaluation, and spread or unpacking. Evaluate each\n  3727\texpression exactly once, in the source-defined order. Errors required\n  3728\tbefore argument evaluation must still occur before it. The record holds\n  3729\tevaluated values; the callee must not reevaluate source expressions.\n  3730\t\n  3731\tAfter source resolution, calls use the common projection and application.\n  3732\tFor a cell-backed callable, the successful path is schematically:\n  3733\t\n  3734\t```clojure\n  3735\t;; UAST notation after source evaluation/resolution and validation.\n  3736\t;; The caller has already established `raise-exit`.\n  3737\t(let [entry (get (cell/get f) :yang.call/entry)\n  3738\t      invocation (assoc evaluated-arguments\n  3739\t                        :yang.call/callee f\n  3740\t                        :yang.call/raise raise-exit)]\n  3741\t  (entry invocation))\n  3742\t```\n  3743\t\n  3744\tThe shared prelude handles projection and validation for cell-backed and\n  3745\timmutable callables. Existing `py/call`, `js/call`, or `php/call` names\n  3746\tmay remain as source call-site helpers, but must not reject an admitted\n  3747\tforeign callable because its native type tag differs.\n  3748\t\n  3749\tThe callee entry installs its exception boundary **before parameter\n  3750\tbinding**. It owns defaults, arity checks, positional/named/variadic\n  3751\tbinding, receiver interpretation, and supported source constraints and\n  3752\tcoercions. It then invokes its body using its private lowered signature\n  3753\tand captured environment.\n  3754\t\n  3755\tEntry boilerplate is not an additional source function activation.\n  3756\tSource recursion limits, tracing, and safepoint call marks must count the\n  3757\toriginal source activation once, not both the entry and its private body\n  3758\tas two source calls. Genuine nested source calls remain observable.\n  3759\t\n  3760\t| Callee example | Interpretation of the same invocation |\n  3761\t|---|---|\n  3762\t| Python function | Python binding/default/keyword rules and captured module globals. A missing required argument raises the Python binding error. |\n  3763\t| JavaScript function | Its receiver mode and argument/default/rest rules. An absent argument yields `undefined` where specified. Nonempty named arguments raise a guest `TypeError`; they are not discarded or converted to a positional object. |\n  3764\t| PHP function | Named-parameter, static-state, reference and declared type rules. Function self comes from `:yang.call/callee`, not the JavaScript receiver. |\n  3765\t| Further frontend | Its declared binding rules, with no new case in the shared prelude or evaluator. |\n  3766\t\n  3767\tA Python unbound function does not gain a first positional argument\n  3768\tbecause a JavaScript member call supplied a receiver. A bound-method entry\n  3769\tsupplies its already-bound self once. An arrow entry retains lexical\n  3770\t`this`. Callables that do not use a receiver ignore its presence.\n  3838\t#### 8.10.7 Normal return and escaping conditions\n  3839\t\n  3840\tNormal return is the value of ordinary Yin application. There is no\n  3841\textra return continuation, completion packet around each result, or new\n  3842\tlanguage-specific return instruction.\n  3843\t\n  3844\tAn escaping source exception invokes the supplied raise exit with:\n  3845\t\n  3846\t```clojure\n  3847\t{:yang.call/reason :yang.call/thrown\n  3848\t :yang.call/value  original-thrown-value}\n  3849\t```\n  3850\t\n  3851\tThis is the argument to an exceptional continuation transfer, not an\n  3852\tordinary return. Returning an identical-looking map returns normal data.\n  3853\tLikewise, a source program throwing a map with these keys throws that\n  3854\tmap as the **payload of a new condition**; its shape is never evidence\n  3855\tthat it is already a condition to forward. No engine-effect value is\n  3856\tsynthesized.\n  3857\t\n  3858\tProtocol failures use qualified reasons such as\n  3859\t`:yang.call/not-callable`, `:yang.call/invalid-invocation`, and\n  3860\t`:yang.call/unsupported-binding`, with plain evidence. The caller maps\n  3861\tthem to a documented local exception form retaining the complete\n  3862\toriginal condition. Callee argument-binding failures retain the actual\n  3863\tcallee exception under `:yang.call/thrown`. These reasons do not extend\n  3864\t`dao.stream`'s outcome algebra.\n  3865\t\n  3866\tThe receiving caller interprets an escaping exception as follows:\n  3867\t\n  3868\t1. A payload valid as an exception in its own runtime retains that\n  3869\t   runtime's ordinary catching and identity behavior.\n  3870\t2. A runtime permitting arbitrary thrown values, such as JavaScript,\n  3871\t   may deliver the original payload directly.\n  3872\t3. A runtime requiring exception objects, such as Python or PHP,\n  3873\t   represents an otherwise foreign payload with its own\n  3874\t   `ForeignException`, retaining the complete condition and original\n  3875\t   payload. Catch-all catches this local object. Typed source catches\n  3876\t   match its local hierarchy, never guessed foreign class names.\n  3877\t\n  3878\t`ForeignException` is a proposed prelude-owned type, not a VM type.\n  3879\tUnchanged rethrow propagates the original condition without adding\n  3880\twrappers. Raising a new exception creates a new condition. Crossing back\n  3881\tinto a runtime recognizing the original exception restores its original\n  3882\ttype and identity. Formatting a foreign payload must not implicitly\n  3883\tinvoke a foreign method.\n  3884\t\n  3885\tThe runtime associates its generated wrapper's identity with the retained\n  3886\tcondition for the wrapper's entire lifetime, separate from source-writable\n  3887\tattributes. The association normally lives in private wrapper-cell\n  3888\tcontents, or an equivalent task-owned identity structure with the same\n  3889\tlifetime. It survives storing or returning the wrapper after the creating\n  3890\tcall/catch activation has ended, and remains recoverable when that same\n  3891\twrapper is rethrown in another activation. Temporary catch state alone is\n  3892\tinsufficient. Reachability/reclamation must retain the condition and its\n  3893\tpayload while the wrapper is live, without an ambient registry.\n  3894\t\n  3895\tThe runtime must not detect wrappers or unwrap arbitrary maps by their\n  3896\tkey names. JavaScript may expose raw payloads for\n  3897\t`:yang.call/thrown`; its local exception for a protocol failure must still\n  3898\tretain that failure's qualified condition. Rethrowing the same generated\n  3899\texception forwards that complete condition, including protocol reasons;\n  3900\tthrowing an unrelated value creates a new `:yang.call/thrown` condition.\n  3901\tSaved activation state refers to this identity-owned association; exiting\n  3902\tor restoring a catch does not destroy it.\n  3903\t\n  3904\tVM application/custody defects and host implementation failures remain\n  3905\toperational failures. Do not catch them broadly and relabel them as\n  3906\tsource exceptions. Existing explicit stream/module refusals keep their\n  3907\tsource mappings under the relevant profiles.\n  3908\t\n  3909\t#### 8.10.8 Cleanup, re-entry, and suspension\n  3910\t\n  3911\tEach activation establishes its own exception bridge and saves its\n  3912\tcallee runtime's dynamic context. Binding, body, and exit run under that\n  3913\tbridge. Before invoking the caller's raise exit, let source catches and\n  3914\tcleanup complete and restore saved handler/depth/frame state. Normal\n  3915\treturn restores it too. A source `finally` replacing a pending return\n  3916\tor exception does so before common propagation under its own rules.\n  3917\t\n  3918\tIn A-to-B-to-A calls, the innermost activation must not jump to an older\n  3919\tA handler past B. Its entry bridge is the boundary: inner A cleanup\n  3920\tprecedes B cleanup, which precedes outer A handling. Every activation\n  3921\towns its saved state and raise exit, without a single current-foreign-\n  3922\thandler cell or ambient dispatcher. Unchanged rethrow preserves the\n  3923\tinner exception as specified in section 8.10.7.\n  3924\t\n  3925\tCleanup runs once per actual exit. Resuming a suspended VM transition\n  3926\tmust not repeat parameter binding, argument evaluation, defaults,\n  3927\treference acquisition, or completed cleanup. The record, continuations,\n  3950\t\n  3951\tLinked modules publish code and immutable specifications. Their bodies\n  3952\tinstantiate function objects and common entries in the importing task,\n  3953\tas section 8.5.6 requires. Install children must not export live function\n  3954\tcells. A higher-order result is an ordinary runtime value, not a new\n  3955\tlinker publication on each call.\n  3956\t\n  3957\tLowering/runtime profiles pin the implementation and binding/value\n  3958\textensions. Existing dependency/effect/custody checks remain. Type facts\n  3959\tstay beside code under `yin.vm.typecheck`, not in lambda language tags.\n  3960\tDo not duplicate existing lambda parameters in another persisted\n  3961\tauthority over the private body signature.\n  3962\t\n  3963\tBefore admitting a compiled composition, its profile/dependency\n  3964\tdeclarations must establish one compatible convention revision for the\n  3965\tentries it exposes. An incompatible revision needs an explicitly pinned\n  3966\tprojection or is refused before execution. Neither a common slot name nor\n  3967\tan opaque runtime closure proves revision compatibility. No runtime\n  3968\tlanguage registry or inspection of captured environments supplies it.\n  3969\t\n  3970\tChanged entry code changes code identities through the normal encoder.\n  3971\tRetain source, lowered UAST, pinned profiles, and projections under\n  3972\texisting provenance/unitarity rules. A fast path may omit the record\n  3973\tonly after proving equivalent evaluation, binding, foreign callability,\n  3974\texceptions, effects, suspension, and identity. Correctness must not\n  3975\tdepend on that optimization.\n  3976\t\n  3977\t#### 8.10.10 Current code and migration\n  3978\t\n  3979\tThe current code records the gap, not a second normative convention:\n  3980\t\n  3981\t| Current implementation | Private call path |\n  3982\t|---|---|\n  3983\t| `yang.python.antlr.prelude` | `py/call-kw` binds arguments and applies the function cell's `:code` to one vector. |\n  3984\t| `yang.typescript.antlr.prelude` | `js/call` checks `:js/type :function` and applies `:code` to receiver and argument vector. |\n  3985\t| `yang.php.antlr.prelude` | `php/call` applies `:code` to the function cell and argument vector; named/reference handling is separate. |\n  3986\t| `test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj` | Demonstrates PHP/TypeScript substrate composition using lookup rewriting and a test-local `js-shaped` wrapper; not proof of this convention. |\n  3987\t\n  3988\tImplementation proceeds as separately verified changes:\n  3989\t\n  3990\t1. Supply the shared guest prelude and fixtures for entry discovery,\n  3991\t   record validation, and condition transfer, using existing bundled/\n  3992\t   linked prelude emitter machinery.\n  3993\t2. Add entries to each supported callable constructor and builtin;\n  3994\t   preserve source metadata/private code while putting binding and\n  3995\t   exception boundaries under the entry. Keep existing source `:call`\n  3996\t   safepoint marks on their original source activation, normally the\n  3997\t   private body; generated entry/binding boilerplate remains unmarked.\n  3998\t   Do not copy a body's source mark onto its entry and count both. A\n  3999\t   separately proved instrumentation transformation may relocate a mark\n  4000\t   only while preserving its source timing and exactly one activation.\n  4001\t3. Route source-resolved calls and callable tests through the convention,\n  4002\t   including supported bound methods, callable objects, recursion, and\n  4003\t   nested runtime re-entry.\n  4004\t4. Integrate explicit export/name projections with module publication\n  4005\t   and task-local instantiation; remove test-only language-pair wrappers\n  4006\t   from the new conformance paths.\n  4007\t5. Pass the acceptance gates before advertising a conforming frontend\n  4008\t   revision. Old body signatures must not be mistaken for new entries.\n  4009\t\n  4010\tA frontend is not conformant while its advertised supported callable\n  4011\tkinds need pairwise wrappers. Legacy non-ANTLR frontends gain no claim\n  4012\tfrom this proposal; joining later requires the same entry obligations.\n  4013\tThere is no implication that current Python, PHP, TypeScript, or a host\n  4014\ttarget has completed this migration.\n  4015\t\n  4016\t#### 8.10.11 Acceptance gates\n  4017\t\n  4018\tExecute real lowered source bodies, not only synthetic functions with\n  4019\tthe expected slot. A harness may compose published exports and namespace\n  4020\tprojections explicitly. It must not rewrite call nodes, extract foreign\n  4021\t`:code`, or insert test-local language-pair wrappers to make calls work.\n  4022\t\n  4023\tRequired evidence:\n  4024\t\n  4025\t- **Local semantics:** existing suites preserve argument order, defaults,\n  4026\t  required/extra arguments, keyword errors, receivers, exceptions, and\n  4027\t  supported reference/static behavior.\n  4028\t- **Direct calls:** each implemented frontend calls every other\n  4029\t  conforming frontend through the same mechanism in both directions\n  4030\t  with compatible values. Derive the test matrix from admitted\n  4031\t  frontends; do not hard-code it into runtime dispatch.\n  4032\t- **Higher-order calls:** pass, return, store, recurse, and call back\n  4033\t  through received functions. Assert identity and captures without\n  4034\t  replacement cells.\n  4035\t- **Callable admission:** source data with an entry-spelled property\n  4036\t  remains data; profile-constructed descriptors are admitted. Exercise\n  4037\t  supported PHP string/array designators both under local resolution and\n  4038\t  explicit callable export, preserving required late lookup without\n  4039\t  foreign namespace guessing.\n  4040\t- **Presence and binding:** distinguish missing from explicit\n  4041\t  null/None/undefined; retain duplicate keywords until binding; supply\n  4042\t  bound self once; evaluate receiver/spreads/defaults at source-defined\n  4043\t  times and counts.\n  4044\t- **Dynamic callability:** where supported, an existing instance becomes\n  4045\t  eligible after its class gains a call method and ineligible after\n  4046\t  deletion. Calls whose arguments change this metadata observe the\n  4047\t  correct source-required lookup time without instance rewrapping.\n  4048\t- **Opaque values:** round-trip foreign objects, functions, strings,\n  4049\t  float64 content, and exact integers unchanged. Assert aliasing and no\n  4050\t  silent rounding/null conversion. Unsupported operations yield\n  4051\t  documented guest errors.\n  4052\t- **References:** absent/incompatible writable-location profiles fail\n  4053\t  before reference acquisition and the body, with no reference-induced\n  4054\t  writeback. Source-required side effects from evaluating arguments still\n  4055\t  occur; rejection must not erase them. Compatible profiles prove\n  4056\t  mutation, aliasing, cleanup, and suspension behavior.\n  4057\t- **Exceptions:** cover binding failures, arbitrary foreign thrown data,\n  4058\t  catch-all/typed catches, unchanged rethrow, replacement in `finally`,\n  4059\t  protocol-error rethrow, condition-shaped thrown maps, and A-to-B-to-A\n  4060\t  propagation. Assert original exception identity and cleanup order,\n  4061\t  not only rendered text. Store/return a generated `ForeignException`,\n  4062\t  finish its creating activation, then rethrow it from another activation\n  4063\t  and assert recovery of the original condition and payload identity.\n  4064\t- **Context lifetime:** repeated success/raise/success, cross-runtime\n  4065\t  recursion, and stream suspension/resume restore handler/depth/frame\n  4066\t  state and perform arguments/cleanup exactly once. Entry boilerplate\n  4067\t  must not double-count source recursion or tracing activations.\n  4068\t- **Publication/custody:** bundled/linked forms agree; two importing\n  4069\t  tasks have independent state. Existing gates still refuse foreign-task\n  4070\t  cells, forged refs, host functions, and inadmissible continuations.\n  4071\t- **Portability:** execute portable packet-to-UAST fixtures on JVM,\n  4072\t  Node, and Dart over the four VMs wherever existing contracts admit\n  4073\t  the program. JVM-only parsers may supply checked-in CST fixtures;\n  4074\t  parser availability is distinct from calling conformance.\n  4075\t\n  4076\tReport callable kinds, binding/value extensions, actual host/VM\n  4077\tcoverage as an explicit host-by-VM matrix, refusals, and remaining gaps\n  4078\tper frontend revision. A scalar example or common UAST grammar alone\n  4105\t  admission/apply machinery); not authentic → ordinary data semantics\n  4106\t  (map lookup). The shape remains a cheap pre-filter for WHEN to ask;\n  4107\t  it never decides meaning. The shared descriptor projection\n  4108\t  (`yang.call/_descriptor`) consults the same way.\n  4109\t- **Interim asymmetry (agreed across the four seats).** The frontend\n  4110\t  kind predicates that route on a `:type :cell-ref` tag before\n  4111\t  dereferencing — TypeScript's `js/cell?` and Python's `py/cell?` —\n  4112\t  still test SHAPE alone at this landing; their owning seats sweep\n  4113\t  them onto `cell/authentic?` in-lane after this merge (TypeScript:\n  4114\t  the `typeof` path and member-call lowering; Python: the three deref\n  4115\t  sites — `py/content-type`, type resolution, `py/eq-objects`). Until\n  4116\t  those sweeps land, a map imitating the reference language is data\n  4117\t  at Clojure positions and at the shared descriptor projection, but\n  4118\t  can still abort at the unswept frontend predicates. Nothing\n  4119\t  currently-correct changes behavior (the widening property holds);\n  4120\t  all-frontends consistency completes at the owning seats' landings.\n  4121\t- **Authentication is not authorization.** An authentic reference\n  4122\t  whose cell lacks an admitted callable entry is not callable; the\n  4123\t  existing not-callable refusal stands. Explicit dereference at a\n  4124\t  reference position (`cell/get`) likewise keeps the strict\n  4125\t  validation boundary — the data fallback lives in call routing.\n  4126\t- **Bearer policy.** An exact copy of a valid reference retains\n  4127\t  bearer behavior: it authenticates, therefore it resolves. No\n  4128\t  construction-history promises are made for identical values. A\n  4129\t  program wanting such a map's fields as data uses explicit lookup at\n  4130\t  a data position.\n  4131\t- **Bag clarification.** A reference stored in data remains a\n  4132\t  reference: storage changes nothing about the value, and what\n  4133\t  decides is the authentication query at the eventual call, not where\n  4134\t  the callee travelled. Equality survives while callability follows\n  4135\t  the heap's answer.\n  4136\t- **Entry-key exception.** \"Ordinary map application always remains\n  4137\t  data\" is true for the cell-ref language, not universally: a map\n  4138\t  carrying `:yang.call/entry` is convention territory whether it\n  4139\t  admits or forges one, and the D2 admission policy (the unadmitted\n  4140\t  entry-shaped map refuses) governs it. That policy is the one\n  4141\t  declared shape-routed exception and is unchanged by this ruling.\n  4142\t- **Static specialization** (a projected-name fast path, say)\n  4143\t  remains an optimization requiring proof of equivalence under\n  4144\t  shadowing and higher-order use, never the routing default: the\n  4145\t  calling contract's pass/return/store/recurse/callback obligations\n  4146\t  (8.10.11) bind every specialization.\n  4147\t\n  4148\t### 8.11 Mutable objects and collections over cells\n  4149\t\n  4150\tThis section records the mutable-objects ruling. A mutable guest object\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
collab/exception-track/1794930000000-engineer-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:24:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc | sed -n '140,225p;2240,2310p;2370,2440p;4070,4105p;4205,4245p;4280,4360p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc | sed -n '890,950p;1040,1135p;1280,1325p;1465,1510p'\"","aggregated_output":"   140\t                     :obj\n   141\t                     (if (not (nil? (get x :php/ref)))\n   142\t                       (php/kind (php/ref-value x))\n   143\t                       (if (data/content= x (data/float64 (data/float-value x)))\n   144\t                         :float\n   145\t                         :int)))))\n   146\t               (if (= (get x :php/type) :array) :array :obj))))))]\n   147\t    [php/int? (fn [x] (= (php/kind x) :int))]\n   148\t    [php/float? (fn [x] (= (php/kind x) :float))]\n   149\t    [php/numk? (fn [k] (if (= k :int) true (= k :float)))]\n   150\t    [php/fl\n   151\t     ;; the host double of an int or float\n   152\t     (fn [x] (if (php/int? x) (integer/to-float x) (data/float-value x)))]\n   153\t    [php/type-name\n   154\t     (fn [k]\n   155\t       (if (= k :string) \"string\"\n   156\t           (if (= k :int) \"int\"\n   157\t               (if (= k :float) \"float\"\n   158\t                   (if (= k :bool) \"bool\"\n   159\t                       (if (= k :null) \"null\"\n   160\t                           (if (= k :array) \"array\" \"mixed\")))))))]\n   161\t\n   162\t    ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------- escapes\n   163\t    ;; The dynamic context is one record in one cell: every capture point\n   164\t    ;; saves it and every escape restores it with :base left alone. The\n   165\t    ;; handler stack is a chain of frames [kind payload rest n depth].\n   166\t    [php/ctx\n   167\t     (fn [handlers depth base frame]\n   168\t       (assoc (assoc (assoc (assoc {} :handlers handlers) :depth depth)\n   169\t                     :base base)\n   170\t              :frame frame))]\n   171\t    [php/restore!\n   172\t     (fn [saved]\n   173\t       (cell/set! php.rt/ctx\n   174\t                  (assoc saved :base (get (cell/get php.rt/ctx) :base))))]\n   175\t    [php/handlers (fn [] (get (cell/get php.rt/ctx) :handlers))]\n   176\t    [php/set-handlers!\n   177\t     (fn [hs] (cell/set! php.rt/ctx (assoc (cell/get php.rt/ctx) :handlers hs)))]\n   178\t    [php/frame-depth (fn [hs] (if (nil? hs) 0 (get hs 3)))]\n   179\t    [php/frame\n   180\t     (fn [kind payload rest]\n   181\t       (php/vconj (php/vconj (php/vconj (php/vconj (php/vconj [] kind) payload) rest)\n   182\t                             (+ 1 (php/frame-depth rest)))\n   183\t                  (get (cell/get php.rt/ctx) :depth)))]\n   184\t    [php/pop-frame\n   185\t     ;; the handler stack below frame hs, at the call depth hs was pushed at\n   186\t     (fn [hs]\n   187\t       (cell/set! php.rt/ctx\n   188\t                  (assoc (assoc (cell/get php.rt/ctx) :handlers (get hs 2))\n   189\t                         :depth (get hs 4))))]\n   190\t    [php/raise\n   191\t     ;; deliver the error to the innermost handler, running each :finally\n   192\t     ;; thunk it unwinds through; the unit root always installs a handler,\n   193\t     ;; so an empty stack is a defect and answers the error\n   194\t     (fn [e]\n   195\t       (let [hs (php/handlers)]\n   196\t         (if (nil? hs)\n   197\t           e\n   198\t           (if (= (get hs 0) :finally)\n   199\t             (do (php/pop-frame hs)\n   200\t                 ((get hs 1) e)\n   201\t                 (php/raise e))\n   202\t             ((get hs 1) e)))))]\n   203\t    [php/unwind-to\n   204\t     ;; pop frames above frame count n, running each finally thunk with null\n   205\t     (fn [n]\n   206\t       (let [hs (php/handlers)]\n   207\t         (if (nil? hs)\n   208\t           