collab/exception-track/1794930000000-engineer-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:14:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'ls collab/exception-track && cat collab/exception-track/1794920000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity.md && tail -n 310 collab/yang-python/1794900000000-reviewer-exception-uniformity-audit.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log'","aggregated_output":"1794920000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity.md\n1794930000000-architect-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\n1794930000000-engineer-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n1794930000000-engineer-exception-ruling-review.kimi-k3.1-pro.stdout.log\n1794930000000-engineer-exception-ruling-review.qwen3.8-max.stdout.log\n1794930000000-review-brief.md\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-07 03:30:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-07 10:30:00 +07 (+0700)\nStatus: OWNER-APPROVED 2026-11-07 — \"enforce everywhere consistently. this should be a separate track.\"\n\n# RULING OF RECORD — the exception track: §8.10.7 enforced uniformly in all four frontends\n\n## The decision\n\nException handling is enforced uniformly across yang.clojure, yang.typescript (js),\nyang.php, and yang.python. Every escaping source exception — from any frontend, through\nany boundary — compiles down to THE SAME continuation: the caller's supplied raise exit\nreceiving the §8.10.7 condition\n\n    {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/thrown, :yang.call/value original-thrown-value}\n\nand every receiving runtime implements the §8.10.7 reception rules (1)–(3) as written.\nThis is a SEPARATE TRACK: it does not ride any frontend's ongoing slice; it has its own\nfolder (`collab/exception-track/`), its own rounds, and its own acceptance gate. The four\nfrontend seats cede these exception-fix surfaces to the track's rounds; the track files\nper-frontend results back into each seat's records.\n\n## The uniformity requirements (each is testable)\n\n**R1 — the callee bridge (js, php are non-compliant today).** Every frontend's published\nfunction entry (and closure/higher-order entry) installs the exception bridge: it reads\nthe supplied `:yang.call/raise` and delivers every escaping source exception AND argument\nbinding failure to it as the §8.10.7 condition. No frontend may let an escaping exception\nfollow the host engine's own handler stack (js today, prelude.cljc:1581), and no frontend\nmay return an uncaught error as an ordinary value (php today, prelude.cljc:190/2384).\n\n**R2 — foreign reception (python, php are non-compliant today).** On receiving a thrown\ncondition: a payload valid in the receiving runtime keeps that runtime's ordinary\ncatching and identity behavior; a runtime permitting arbitrary thrown values (js) may\ndeliver the payload raw; a runtime requiring exception objects (python, php) represents a\nforeign payload with its own generated `ForeignException`, retaining the complete\ncondition and original payload — catch-all catches it, typed catches match the LOCAL\nhierarchy, never guessed foreign class names. Raising the raw envelope (php:2384) or the\nraw payload (python:1034) is non-compliant.\n\n**R3 — protocol failures retain the condition.** `:yang.call/not-callable`,\n`:invalid-invocation`, `:unsupported-binding` map to a documented local exception form\nretaining the complete original condition, so an unchanged rethrow can forward it. A fresh\nlocal error containing only rendered text (js:1627, python:1046) is non-compliant.\n\n**R4 — clojure unifies.** The internal outcome-token channel\n(clojure.cljc:1110/1486) may remain an optimization ONLY with a proof of equivalence at\nevery boundary; absent that proof it unifies onto the raise-exit path. Clojure's `try`\nrejection of `finally` and its single-catch restriction are source-semantics decisions\nTHIS RULING DOES NOT CHANGE — R4 is about the continuation, not clojure's catch surface.\n\n**R5 — no new shape-routing.** Nothing in the track may route on the condition map's\nshape beyond the §8.10.7 contract; a lookalike map stays ordinary data (the cell-ref\nruling's rule, restated). No synthesized engine-effect exception values.\n\n## What the audit establishes (the baseline)\n\nThe gpt-6.1-sol audit (collab/yang-python/1794900000000, verdict DIVERGENT, HEAD\ne9a0b430) is the track's baseline evidence: python hooks the raise exit (R1 compliant);\njs and php lack the bridge (R1); python and php lack ForeignException (R2); js and python\nlose protocol conditions (R3); clojure has the private channel (R4). Zero cross-language\n§8.10.7 legs exist in the conformance kit today.\n\n## Acceptance gate\n\nThe conformance kit (`test/yang/call/conformance.clj`) grows exception legs — the\n§8.10.11 exception row as acceptance: binding failures, arbitrary foreign thrown data,\ncatch-all/typed catches, unchanged rethrow, finally replacement, protocol-error rethrow,\ncondition-shaped thrown maps staying data, A→B→A propagation with identity and cleanup\norder, and the stored-ForeignException rethrown-from-another-activation case. The track\nis DONE when every admitted pair passes those legs with real source on both sides, and\nthe divergences R1–R4 are each closed by a focused test pinning the compliant behavior.\n\n## Sequencing\n\nArchitect review of this ruling, then an engineer mob review, then per-frontend fix\nrounds (smallest first: R3 js/python, R1 php, R2 python/php, R4 clojure), the conformance\nlegs landing alongside the fixes they gate. The known-gaps row for this track is filed\nfrom the audit's divergence list.\n\n— filed by the yang.python orchestrator seat on the owner's instruction\n{\"type\":\"thread.started\",\"thread_id\":\"01a1b1c4-7ea1-7c83-938d-c4ba15a3803d\"}\n{\"type\":\"turn.started\"}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_0\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’ll read the architecture rules, then trace exception lowering, call boundaries, and test evidence in the specified worktree. I’ll keep the audit read-only and avoid running tests.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"pwd && git status --short && rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*yang.antlr*' -g '*datom.world.md'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"pwd && git status --short && rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*yang.antlr*' -g '*datom.world.md'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-euV8Sa40' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-06 23:50:20.656 xcodebuild[20787:113049836]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-06 23:50:21.048 xcodebuild[20787:113049835] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-Qld1dRlb' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-06 23:50:22.535 xcodebuild[20809:113049914]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-06 23:50:22.930 xcodebuild[20809:113049908] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\nAGENTS.md\\ndocs/design/datom.world.md\\ndocs/design/yang.antlr.md\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat AGENTS.md && cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat AGENTS.md && cat docs/design/datom.world.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# ==============================================================================\\n# datom.world – Development Rules & Architectural Foundations\\n# ==============================================================================\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.world.md`](./docs/design/datom.world.md) for master architecture, core philosophy, foundational axioms, non-negotiable invariants, code style, development workflow, and operational guides (testing, team roles, agent delegation, website formats).\\n---\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\n---\\n\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\n\\n## Core Philosophy\\n\\nEverything is data.\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\n\\n### Foundational Axioms\\n\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\n   At system scale, a \\\"system\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\n\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\n\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\n\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\n\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\n\\n## Streams\\n\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\n\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\nthe stream's own.\\n\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\n\\n### Host Boundaries\\n\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\n\\n## Architectural Commitments\\n\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\n\\n## Design Principles\\n\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\n\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\n\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\n  ordinary streams.\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\n  auto-index observer.\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\n\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\nretired `:global` tag in\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\n\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\n\\n## Code Style\\n\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\n\\n## Development Workflow\\n\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\n  leaves to review.\\n\\n### Problem Decomposition\\n\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\n2. Identify invariants.\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\n\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\n\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg --files | rg '(\\\"'^|/)(clojure.cljc|lower.cljc|prelude.cljc|.*(exception|conformance).*|AGENTS.md)$'\\\"'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg --files | rg '(\\\"'^|/)(clojure.cljc|lower.cljc|prelude.cljc|.*(exception|conformance).*|AGENTS.md)$'\\\"'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"AGENTS.md\\ntest/fixtures/codec/transit/exceptions.edn\\ntest/fixtures/codec/cbor/exceptions.edn\\ntest/dao/host/conformance_test.cljc\\ntest/dao/jing/cbor_conformance_test.cljc\\ntest/dao/space/datomic_query_conformance_test.cljc\\ntest/dao/stream/conformance.cljc\\ncollab/orch-lane/1793660000000-engineer-p4-yangcall-conformance.gpt-6-luna.stdout.log\\ncollab/orch-lane/1793810000000-engineer-p4-conformance-so-r2.gpt-6-luna.stdout.log\\ncollab/orch-lane/1793790000000-engineer-p4-conformance-so.gpt-6-luna.stdout.log\\ncollab/yang-python/1792205000000-note-to-yang-python-orchestrator-conformance.md\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\ncollab/yang-call/1793610000000-orchestrator-to-ts-php-crosslang-conformance-gap.md\\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc\\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc\\nsrc/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc\\nsrc/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/lower.cljc\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc\\ncollab/orchestration/1790857898202-subagent-license-gpl2-exception.claude-opus-5-5.report-r2.md\\ncollab/orchestration/1790857898202-subagent-license-gpl2-exception.claude-opus-5-5.stdout-r2.log\\ncollab/orchestration/1790857898202-subagent-license-gpl2-exception.claude-opus-5-5.report.md\\ncollab/or