nd\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:35:- [yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md): Universal AST content and projection discipline.\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:36:- [yin.vm.semantic.md](./yin.vm.semantic.md): A identity, semantic execution and A→R derivation.\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:55:**A is the only code identity.** R is the register image checksum. The\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:57:artifact with no identity in `dao.jing`, no content address, and no\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:73:under the baseline profile. This is not ClojureScript-to-wasm. Toolchain pins\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:79:The mandatory feature profile is **core wasm 1.0 with mutable globals and the original function\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:90:Every extension requires a separate named profile, W0 validation/probe on all target engines and\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:112:- The October amendments to the register and semantic designs make **A the code identity**. R is the register image checksum. Existing `:hash`, `:image`, and offset rows still use R for native compatibility. Do not publish wasm binaries as a new code identity, fetch code by a wasm checksum, or bypass A-to-R admission. Retain access to the Universal AST through existing provenance/addressing.\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:135:- Current A identity and A→R admission above govern over the older Rust topology and R-as-identity wording (§§3, 7.5). R remains the exact executable checksum/offset-row token. Neither backend creates an identity or bypasses canonical admission.\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:136:- Rust K-D2b uses u32 indices and qualified host limits (§4.2 and §11 K-D2b); wasm keeps 64-bit **wire pc fields**, not a promise of unrestricted native execution. The internal facade checks every pc/operand/attachment against the Rust domain and declared limits before conversion or mutation and returns the same qualified `:host-limit`. W0 records the common accepted profile; tests outside it require declared refusals, never truncation or a second widened Rust Machine. Wide register tests above 65,535 still fit u32.\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:146:scalar profile fixes only this lane's supported domain, not all future Rust profiles (questions\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:214:### 2.3 Module imports, exports and deterministic emission\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:233:profile yield byte-identical WAT and assembled `.wasm` binaries across runs and platforms.\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:364: :image identity-of-row-containing-resume-pc\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:455:section. The baseline preallocates table capacity (W0 test profile: 65,536 slots) and uses host\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:458:must pass the W0 probe on each runtime and must not change the baseline module feature profile.\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:476:derivation/checksum, wasm layout/emitter version and feature profile; R alone is not executable\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:481:target/profile, translator and internal-layout versions in the cache key alongside the admitted A/R\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:501:- **W0 — Oracle and feasibility:** export/extend K0 from CLJS first; freeze admitted images/R/traces/refusals; probe crate wasm32 compilation, linker/profile, internal layout and stream-adapter bounds. Pin deterministic emitter/assembler. No host-service fallback.\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:506:- **W5 — Complete lane:** all 23 opcodes and complete W1–W5 full traces on Node, Wasmtime and Wasmer; deterministic binaries and external import/profile audit. Wasmer's formerly W4 full-suite gate is superseded by this owner-directed schedule, not removed.\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:528:- **Canonical admission and provenance.** Bare hand images remain useful under the existing raw kernel API; production module execution must retain A derivation verification. Compiled wasm caches are disposable artifacts. They cannot become another authority for code or continuation identity.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:67:6. **Immutable identity.** Pin the full *checking configuration* for each checking result: the checked declaration environment, term graph, compiled kernel artifact, VM/compiler/schema versions, and the runtime trust identity of the closed executable composition the checker runs under (section 5).\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:87:| Hash/identity of canonical terms | Important for performance | Sound structural comparison before trusting cache shortcuts |\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:107:#### Checking configuration and runtime trust identity\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:109:A checking result must not be identified by its listed ingredients alone. At run time the checker resolves every name through a fixed lookup chain — lexical bindings, program store, primitives, then the composition-supplied module registry (`resolve-var` in `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`) — and a host module is a declared boundary, not a content-addressed one: registration checks the profile, not the code, and a host module's address is nil because the host is where the code already is (`src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc`). Two runs can therefore present identical listed result identifiers while executing different trusted operation implementations.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:114:- The closed set of linked modules and their identities: content addresses for fetched code, and for host modules an explicit **host attestation** — module name, exported symbols, profile records, and the host implementation version the publishing composition answers for. Where content addressing is unavailable, the attestation and version are the identity.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:120:- The configuration is part of result and cache identity; results and cache entries are shared only between runs with the same configuration.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:128:Define tagged logical constructors, projected onto the universal graph/tuple model:\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:140:These are **logical term tags** and may be represented as datom attributes/relationships in the existing UAST. Do not confuse them with Yin's runtime instruction opcodes, even when both use the same data substrate. A translation/projection can use common interned nodes when semantics permit.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:172:A specification change mints a new spec version: it changes the checking-configuration identity (section 5) and invalidates previously issued checking artifacts.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:180:- Tagged term constructors and projections.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:187:**Acceptance:** Terms round-trip through the datom/UAST projection, preserve binding, and cannot reference invalid environment versions.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:204:theorem identity : (P : Prop) → P → P :=\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:210:**Acceptance:** The dependent identity proof is accepted and malformed variants are rejected — each judged against exactly the judgments the frozen Gate S specification defines, with rejections citing the judgment violated. This is not yet a complete CIC kernel; acceptance covers the v1 spec fragment and nothing beyond it.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:221:- Sound memoization keyed to **every judgment input**: the compared terms, the local typing context including its local definitions, the semantic substitution environment for open terms, the global environment identity, and the reduction policy (transparency and admitted unfoldings). Identical de Bruijn syntax under different local definitions can reduce differently, so term syntax plus global environment alone is not a sound key. In v1, conversion caching is restricted to closed terms under a pinned global environment and reduction policy; caching open-term results additionally requires the full local context and semantic environment in the key.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:263:- Cache checks by checking-configuration identity (section 5) with sound collision/identity handling.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:272:- Implement the exact kernel features required by each target corpus: universe rules, proof irrelevance, inductive computation, quotients, projections, literals, transparency, etc.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:276:**Acceptance:** Publish a precisely bounded subset of Lean exports that Yin.CIC independently rechecks with matching outcomes. Full Lean compatibility is not assumed.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:282:  term.cljc          ; logical terms and projections\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:344:1. Pick a tiny source subset supported by `yang.clojure`: `fn`, application, `if`, `let`, and deterministic data projections.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:363:- How will compiler/VM semantic changes invalidate previous checking artifacts? The mechanism is checking-configuration identity (section 5); the residual decision is the reissue policy for results issued under an older configuration.\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:378:**Bottom line:** Write the checker in Clojure, compile it using Yang, and run it as a Yin program. Keep the logical checker and the proof term semantics explicit; freeze the trusted code image and environment; audit the Yang/Yin trust boundary; and validate the design with a minimal dependent identity proof before tackling Lean compatibility.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:27:- The parser, elaborator, checker, and any execution adapter are programs. When compiled for Yin, their executable UAST follows the existing VM projection and segment contracts.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:52:The need for an interpretation identity follows from the axiom; the particular closed notation selected for v1 is a product decision.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:118:A content hash is an identity, not a retention mechanism. Publication must retain the referenced artifacts, not merely their hashes.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:126:This is an engineering constraint, not an impossibility theorem. “Community-extensible syntax” here means that ordinary libraries may introduce new concrete notation, fixity, binding forms, or interpretation procedures beyond a frozen profile. A fixed extension mechanism can support bounded combinations, but its effective notation environment still becomes an interpretation input.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:131:| Mathematical surface and a closed grammar | Parsing and lowering can be frozen and audited | New conventions require a new profile or frontend |\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:150:| Source admission | Source bytes and explicit request profile | Retained source artifact and request record | Validates encoding and request shape |\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:156:Each emitted artifact and each derivation record is content-addressed. A transport cursor or retry identifier is an occurrence coordinate, not the content identity of a term.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:182:Term content is not normalized merely to improve hash sharing. Structural identity and logical definitional equality remain different relations. Any term canonicalization must be specified and tested independently of proof checking.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:188:For executable programs, [the semantic VM contract](./yin.vm.semantic.md) remains authoritative: the canonical register-shaped segment address **A** is the executable code identity. AST row addresses, logical term roots, and source artifact addresses must not be relabeled as A.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:190:No Tao-specific instruction, evaluator mode, or alternative segment identity is introduced.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:214:The first mathematical example is dependent identity: the proposition \\\\(\\\\Pi(P:\\\\mathrm{Prop}).\\\\,P\\\\to P\\\\), with the explicit proof \\\\(\\\\lambda(P:\\\\mathrm{Prop}).\\\\,\\\\lambda(h:P).\\\\,h\\\\). Its validity comes from Gate S’s predicative theory, not from resemblance to Lean notation.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:230:The notation profile must freeze token boundaries, punctuation, comments, qualified-name syntax, universe spelling, and any ASCII aliases before parser acceptance. These choices cannot remain host-library defaults.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:236:The notation profile pins Unicode handling and the identifier repertoire. Identifier normalization is explicit: v1 requires the selected normalized spelling and rejects nonconforming identifiers instead of silently changing them. Original bytes remain retained even on rejection.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:273:- Frontend and notation-profile identity.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:289:Cache identity includes the inputs and configuration of the cached stage. An earlier source-level receipt cannot certify a new interpretation merely because the final term happens to share an address. Any reuse of a narrower kernel result must remain distinguishable from evidence connecting written notation to that term.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:291:Addresses establish content identity. They do not prove that the implementation is correct, that a host attestation is trustworthy, or that artifacts remain available.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:301:Occurrence identity includes origin, root, and structural path. A content address alone is insufficient: repeated identical subexpressions can have different source locations and binding contexts.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:338:The explicit statement renderer is itself an interpretation dependency for human review. Its identity must be recorded when its output is used as audit evidence. It must not hide arguments or restore ambiguous notation by default.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:368:Publishing a new frontend or notation profile does not mutate an existing profile. Reinterpreting old source under the new profile produces new evidence. A consumer explicitly chooses which interpretation it accepts.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:379:| E3 — Checking composition | Candidate medium connected to the CIC checker under pinned configurations | Dependent identity accepted and specified invalid variants rejected, after Gate S and the required CIC milestones |\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:411:- Interpretation identity accompanies checking identity.\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:422:The notation profile must settle remaining concrete spellings before E0 closes. Wire schemas and event layouts require a focused review against existing row and stream contracts; this note does not add speculative slots to canonical VM structures.\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:9:progress through the linked inclusion profile, dynamic scope, namespaces,\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:41:  php-src selection, the full permutation corpus and the linked profile (P4\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:72:revision and profile keys; the final naming at acceptance is the owner's.\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:92:parsing with three-host execution, the linked-profile matrix of bundled on\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:193:- **The dynamic-scope profile**: a function whose body contains\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:236:  and value; objects by property, or by identity for `===`.\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:361:scope, so it forces the dynamic-scope profile on its function. It is refused\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:599:| 6  | `$x =& $x`, or the same slot on    | no-op: the identity check precedes |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1462:- **Worker**: `yang.antlr.cst/make-worker` unchanged, profile `{:grammar-id\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1500:under a ticks profile only (P7-b).\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1533:|            | `$$x` (dynamic profile, P9),   | writes through `php/assign`    |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1664:|                          | `extract` (dynamic profile);         |            |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1772:### 10.1 Value encoding (runtime profile data)\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1880:  function is on the dynamic-scope profile (at top level it is `$GLOBALS`).\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1912:   without concrete segment pins\\\"), not solved here (owner decision B8.10).\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1929:  nondeterministic: the profile offers a deterministic **safepoint-count\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1997:|       | profile, CST export of modes     | a heredoc with indentation        |\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2015:|       | and linked profiles, the        